strace -- version 6.8

Captured in the prune image (docker/prune_phase/layers/Dockerfile on the run-phase image), arm64,
Linux 7.0 (Docker Desktop), Landlock ABI 8, as root in an ordinary container with --network none:

  strace -f -qq -y -s 4096 -o basic-full.txt \
    -e trace=%file,%network,%process,%desc,landlock_restrict_self,setsid,setpgid,ioctl \
    phobos.sh --config <[read] and [execute] on /usr, /lib, /bin> -- \
    bash -c 'cat /etc/hostname; mkdir /etc/x; touch "/tmp/a\"b<newline>c"; exec 3<>/dev/tcp/10.0.0.1/80'

basic.txt keeps 127 of its lines, unchanged and in their order: the network layer's port-only
enforcer (its execve with --no-filesystem, its landlock_restrict_self and the connect guard's
refusals of its own nscd lookups), the clone that started the filesystem layer's process, and for
that process and the processes it started every execve, clone, socket, connect, mkdirat and
landlock_restrict_self line and every refused call, with each <unfinished ...> half kept exactly
when its <... resumed> half is.

AT_FDCWD is decorated: -y prints it as AT_FDCWD</var/tmp/testing-dir>, so a relative path is
resolved against the decoration and the per-process working directory is only a fallback.

Sockets are decorated as <socket:[inode]> under -y, without the protocol, so a socket's family and
type come from the socket() call that returned the descriptor.

-qq suppresses the "+++ exited with" lines; none appear in the capture.

--seccomp-bpf is not used: strace's filter answers SECCOMP_RET_TRACE, the connect guard's filter
answers SECCOMP_RET_USER_NOTIF for the calls it supervises, which takes precedence, and the guard's
refusal of the connect to 10.0.0.1 was missing from a trace taken with it. Measured beside it: a
traced run of the same chain around /bin/cat took 5.8 s without it, 2.3 s with it, and 0.6 s
untraced.
